# Git practice

Follow this whenever you create a branch or write a commit message.

## Branch names

Use `feature/<feature-name>`: a `feature/` prefix, then a short kebab-case name for the work.

```text
feature/feature-name
feature/auth
```

- One branch per feature.
- Keep the name short and specific (`feature/auth`, `feature/categories`).
- Create the branch before committing feature work.

## Before a new branch

Pull `{{BASE_BRANCH}}` in that repository, then create the feature branch from the updated `{{BASE_BRANCH}}`.

```text
git checkout {{BASE_BRANCH}}
git pull origin {{BASE_BRANCH}}
git checkout -b feature/<feature-name>
```

- Do this in every repository you branch, including the parent, client, and server.
- Pull `{{BASE_BRANCH}}` even when the user did not ask for a pull as a separate step.
- Stop if `{{BASE_BRANCH}}` has uncommitted changes, or if `feature/<feature-name>` already exists. Do not reset or reuse it.

## Commit messages

The description lists the precise changes as bullet points. Each bullet is one concrete change.

```text
- added categories feature
- implemented categories UI page
```

- One change per bullet.
- Name what was added, implemented, or fixed.
- Skip vague lines such as "updates" or "misc fixes".
- Pass the message with a HEREDOC. Do not commit unless the user asked.

```text
git commit -m "$(cat <<'EOF'
- added categories feature
- implemented categories UI page

EOF
)"
```
